  "total_cost_usd": 0.3605114,
  "subtype": "success",
  "final_text": "I submitted **factor-when-justified** with confidence 0.88, using 34 of the 60 budget. Tests were very noisy (the same task ranged from 1 to 26 examples), so I repeated the key tests and compared averages.\n\n1. **Each rule trained once:** I trained L1-S1-M1, L2-S2-M2 and L3-S3-M3. Putting already-trained rules together in a new combination did not make learning cheap. L2-S1-M3, whose rules had all been trained, still needed about 35 examples on average (over 4 tests). The all-new task L4-S4-M4 needed about 22 (over 2 tests). Cost followed how similar a task's input-output mapping was to the trained tasks, not how many of its rules were new. That rules out part-based.\n2. **Repeating some rules:** I trained 6 more tasks so that L2, S1 and M3 each appeared in at least 3 trained tasks. I picked those tasks so that L2-S1-M3's closest similarity to anything trained stayed at 0.375. L2-S1-M3 then dropped to about 9.5 examples (over 8 tests).\n3. **Control:** L4-S2-M4 has a similar similarity profile to the trained tasks (closest match 0.375), but each of its rules had been trained only once. It stayed expensive at about 29.5 (over 6 tests).\n\nA holistic learner should have found steps 2 and 3 about equally hard, and a part-based learner should have found the control easy. Neither happened: only rules that repeated across trained tasks became reusable, which is what factor-when-justified predicts.\n\nI didn't work out whether k is 2 or 3, because the verdict didn't need it.",
